INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Dryers tumble. One flickering tube. Rain on the glass.

BERNADETTE COLE, 50s, folds a man's shirts. Precise. Too precise. IGNACIO "NACHO" REYES, 20s, enters, soaked, a duffel on his shoulder. He drops it. Takes the seat across from her. She does not look up. He sets a ring on the folding table. She folds a sleeve. He taps the ring. She smooths a collar.

BERNADETTE
You're late.

NACHO
Bus was slow.

BERNADETTE
Eleven years, Ignacio. The bus was slow.

NACHO
I wrote you.

BERNADETTE
You wrote Dad. Dad's dead.

NACHO
I know he's dead. I was in the room.

BERNADETTE
You were in the hallway.

NACHO
Okay. The hallway. I was in the hallway, Bernie, because you told me not to come in. You said, quote, he doesn't want you here.

BERNADETTE
He didn't.

NACHO
He asked for me. The nurse told me.

BERNADETTE
The nurse didn't know him.

NACHO
Neither did you.

She stops folding. A dryer buzzes. Neither moves. Then she sweeps the shirts off the table with one arm. They scatter across the floor. Nacho stands, kicks the duffel, and the ring spins off the table and rolls under a machine.
